<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="lysine degradation III" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWYQT-1" dataSource="PlantCyc" />
  <Description>This pathway shares beginning steps with |FRAME: LYSINE-DEG2-PWY| up to the molecule glutaryl-CoA. In |FRAME: LYSINE-DEG2-PWY| crotonyl-CoA is subsequently generated from (E)-glutaconyl-CoA by enzyme EC 4.1.1.70. However, no such enzyme is known from plants. Recent studies in Arabidopsis instead showed that degradation of lysine leads to the generation of D-2-hydroxyglutarate |CITS: [23718121], [20501910], [24561575]|, which was subsequently converted to 2-oxoglutarate (shown in this pathway) by a known enzyme D-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase |CITS: [19586914], [21296880]|. The pathway starts in the cytosol and requires an unknown mitochondrial membrane transporter. The Arabidopsis mitochondrial D-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase is stereospecific. It is a homodimer, contains one FAD per monomer |CITS: [19586914]|. An electron acceptor is needed for the enzyme activity. The purified enzyme had no activity with NAD(P) or cytochrome c |CITS: [19586914]|. The in-vivo electron acceptor is the ETF-ETFQO complex located at the mitochondrial inner membrane which then pass the electron onto the respiratory chain. The product 2-oxoglutarate enters the TCA cycle.</Description>
